WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.420
Researchers identified clot in four as the receptor that lets a common gut bacterium talks and damage colon cells and promote tumors. This is Planetarian Daily, Episode 231. Liver tumors that begin without driver mutations, a cholesterol handling root in liver cells, and a giant virus carrying a host potassium channel follow from there. A team at the Johns Hopkins Kimmel Cancer Center ran a genome-wide CRISPR screen in colon epithelial cells with the findings published in Nature.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.040
temperature. Turning off the Claudein IV gene, stopped Bacteriode's

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.560
fragileus toxin from attaching to those cells. Structural biologists confirmed that the toxin

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:43.920
and Claudein IV form a direct complex. Claudein IV is the protein that maintains tight

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.880
junctions between neighboring cells. Maxwell White led the CRISPR screen in collaboration

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:54.500
with Matthew Waldor's laboratory at Harvard Medical School. A molecular decoy built from

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.040
the binding interaction protected mice from toxin damage and tests.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.740
The bacterium lives in roughly 20% of healthy people, and only certain strains make the

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:05.740
toxin tied to inflammation and tumors.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:12.440
The same group showed in 2009 that toxin-producing strains promote colon tumors through chronic inflammation.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:18.140
Senior author Cynthia Sears noted that pinning down the receptor opens roots to new detection

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:23.260
and therapy strategies for diarrhea, colorectal cancer, and bloodstream infections.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:29.214
Most people assume bacterial toxins latch onto signaling receptors that cells use to take

00:01:29.214 --> 00:01:32.014
in outside messages and fire internal responses.

00:01:32.654 --> 00:01:38.034
Certain bacteriodes, fragilist strains, instead bind clot in four, a protein whose primary

00:01:38.034 --> 00:01:41.794
role is forming the physical seals between neighboring intestinal cells.

00:01:42.274 --> 00:01:47.854
That binding lets the toxin reach and cleave e-catherin, the molecule that holds the gut barrier

00:01:47.854 --> 00:01:48.334
together.

00:01:48.994 --> 00:01:52.934
Once that adhesive protein is cut, the lining becomes leaky and inflamed.

00:01:52.934 --> 00:01:56.994
And every person carries the same structural protein in their colon lining.

00:01:57.414 --> 00:02:01.694
The toxin is treating a junction brick as a docking port, not a message receiver.

00:02:02.294 --> 00:02:05.834
The team then built a molecular decoy that mimics the binding site.

00:02:06.294 --> 00:02:10.674
In mice, that decoy intercepted the toxin before it reached real cell junctions.

00:02:11.254 --> 00:02:15.634
Future experiments will need to determine whether the same decoy strategy works in people

00:02:15.634 --> 00:02:18.114
without disrupting normal tight junction function.

00:02:18.114 --> 00:02:24.434
A working decoy would block toxin-driven colon damage without removing the bacterium from the gut entirely.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:31.590
YAP-1 drives hepatocelular carcinoma through DNA-D methylation rather than canonical gene mutations.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:39.590
Researchers submitted the work highlighted on the longevity subreddit, showing that epigenetic mechanisms alone can start liver cancer.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:46.210
The result challenges the assumption that driver gene mutations are always required for hepatocelular carcinoma.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:51.190
The finding broadens how tumors can form when mutation-driven pathways are absent.

00:02:52.252 --> 00:02:57.252
High dietary cholesterol activates the ral protein in liver cells, which then triggers

00:02:57.252 --> 00:03:03.012
ketepsin A to reduce LDL receptors, in work published in Nature from UC San Diego.

00:03:03.712 --> 00:03:08.972
Blocking ketepsin A with a small molecule inhibitor stabilizes those receptors and lowered circulating

00:03:08.972 --> 00:03:09.972
LDL in mice.

00:03:10.612 --> 00:03:15.752
Senior author Alan Saltiel explained that the liver normally clears LDL cholesterol through

00:03:15.752 --> 00:03:22.012
LDL receptors on cell surfaces. Experiments combine mouse models with human cell studies to map how

00:03:22.012 --> 00:03:28.032
RAL activity leads to receptor loss. An existing cathepsin A inhibitor, a CTSA inhibitor,

00:03:28.292 --> 00:03:33.052
had already been evaluated in a phase one trial for heart failure before that program ended.

00:03:33.958 --> 00:03:40.358
The newly traced RAL's CTSA route operates separately from statin and PCSK-9 targets already in use.

00:03:40.938 --> 00:03:45.638
An inhibitor previously tested for safety in people could shorten the usual timeline to a phase

00:03:45.638 --> 00:03:50.478
to efficacy trial. If the approach holds in humans, it would give clinicians an option

00:03:50.478 --> 00:03:55.458
for patients who do not reach target levels with current drugs. What remains to be shown

00:03:55.458 --> 00:04:00.598
is whether the same CTSA inhibitor lowers LDL without new side effects when given at the

00:04:00.598 --> 00:04:05.978
doses needed for lipid control. Prior phase 1 safety data on that inhibitor provides a

00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:11.318
concrete starting point rather than requiring an entirely new compound. Further mouse work will

00:04:11.318 --> 00:04:16.138
need to confirm that receptor stabilization translates into sustained LDL reduction over

00:04:16.138 --> 00:04:17.018
longer periods.

00:04:17.978 --> 00:04:23.598
An algal virus genome contains a tandem potassium channel gene acquired from its host, according

00:04:23.598 --> 00:04:25.418
to a report from FIS.org.

00:04:25.858 --> 00:04:30.938
The discovery supports the idea that giant viruses have transferred genes that help build complex

00:04:30.938 --> 00:04:33.178
membrane proteins in early eukaryotes.

00:04:33.658 --> 00:04:36.898
The work appears in proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences.

00:04:36.898 --> 00:04:41.438
diseases. The finding adds evidence that viruses contributed to the evolution of sophisticated

00:04:41.438 --> 00:04:47.764
ion channels still used by modern cells. Inferno-Gallus' jammacy is a small, overwrapped

00:04:47.764 --> 00:04:53.204
or a sore whose jaw muscles were adapted from mashing soft fruit. Paleontologists describe

00:04:53.204 --> 00:04:57.604
the Cretaceous fine from North Dakota as the earliest known dinosaur with this feeding

00:04:57.604 --> 00:05:03.884
strategy, tied to the rise of flowering plants. The species belongs to a group of small feathered

00:05:03.884 --> 00:05:06.764
dinosaurs that lived alongside early angiosperms.

00:05:07.324 --> 00:05:12.584
Jaw muscle reconstruction revealed adaptations for processing soft, energy-rich plant material

00:05:12.964 --> 00:05:14.984
rather than harder vegetation or meat.

00:05:15.956 --> 00:05:20.596
Two curved, pie-conjugated molecules linked at three points create an internal cavity

00:05:20.596 --> 00:05:23.376
that selectively binds shape-matched planar guests.

00:05:24.196 --> 00:05:29.116
Chemists form the cage through a precise face-to-face connection that leaves a defined space inside

00:05:29.116 --> 00:05:29.716
the structure.

00:05:29.716 --> 00:05:34.616
Binding tests showed specificity for molecules with matching flat geometry.

00:05:35.076 --> 00:05:39.476
The design offers a new route to host guest chemistry for sensing or separation.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:44.750
Volunteers use ultrasonic detectors after dark to record echolocation calls

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:47.650
and help map bad activity and species distribution.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:51.670
The approach gives researchers data on nocturnal insect control

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:54.390
and habitat use that would be hard to gather otherwise.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:58.490
Participants learn to recognize the distinct chirps produced by different

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:04.090
microbat species during flight. The project also changes how people perceive bats once they

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:09.456
experience the animals through sound rather than sight alone. Engineers link separate quantum

00:06:09.456 --> 00:06:14.536
chips with four superconducting cables that preserve fragile quantum states across the connection.

00:06:15.336 --> 00:06:19.936
The cables have to carry quantum information without introducing decoherence that would destroy

00:06:19.936 --> 00:06:25.896
the computational state. The modular approach is one path towards scaling systems beyond single chip

00:06:25.896 --> 00:06:30.696
limits. The interconnection is a practical step for building larger distributed quantum

00:06:30.696 --> 00:06:36.596
machines. A kerosene-based gel developed at Concordia University allows easier storage

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:41.716
and flow control for rocket and satellite propulsion. One family's involvement reported

00:06:41.716 --> 00:06:48.236
by KOSU helped move gene therapy research forward at a critical stage. The past seven days also

00:06:48.236 --> 00:06:53.236
ran a cavity-stopping liquid through early pediatric testing, put drought-avoiding plants

00:06:53.236 --> 00:06:57.376
at Uluru against extreme heat, and tested Inulin against knee pain.

00:06:58.016 --> 00:07:03.036
A liquid painted on teeth move from lab data into early pediatric testing as a way to stop

00:07:03.036 --> 00:07:05.136
cavities without drilling or anesthesia.

00:07:05.876 --> 00:07:10.456
Collaborative work on desert plants at Uluru showed drought avoiding species handled extreme

00:07:10.456 --> 00:07:12.336
heat better than drought-tolerating ones.

00:07:12.976 --> 00:07:18.056
A daily Inulin fiber supplement reduced knee osteoarthritis pain and improved grip strength

00:07:18.056 --> 00:07:21.296
by shifting gut bacteria and raising GLP1 levels.

